  "worked_solution": "The first three terms of the binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+\u22ef.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=-9k$ and $n=7$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1-9k)}^{7} & =1+7\\cdot (-9k)+\\frac{7(7-1)}{2!}\\cdot (-9k)^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =1-63k+\\frac{7\\cdot 6}{2}\\cdot 81{k}^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =1-63k+21\\cdot 81{k}^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =1-63k+1\\,701{k}^{2}+\u22ef\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
